  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure continues to ridge in from southeastern Ontario\nand the eastern Great Lakes Region this morning with cool temps\nand some patchy fog especially north and west of the Capital\nDistrict with the clear skies and light to calm winds. The\nradiative mist/fog should burn off quickly with the strong\nsubsidence associated with the anticyclone for seasonable temps\nnear to slightly below normal for late August. Max temps were\naccepted close to the NBM with highs in the mid to upper 70s in\nthe major valleys and mid 60s to lower 70s over the hills and\nmtns. Another cool night is expected with lows in the upper 40s\nto mid 50s, but some mid and high clouds will increase Sat \nnight into Sun morning. \n\nIn the west northwest flow aloft, a short-wave will bring some \nisolated-scattered showers and a few thunderstorms across the \nwestern Adirondacks and a separate batch south of the Capital \nRegion during the afternoon on Sunday. We expanded the coverage \nof showers/isolated thunderstorms across the entire forecast\narea Sunday night, as the short-wave moves through. If faster \ntrends continue on the CAMs and short-range guidance, then we \nmay have to expand the coverage of scattered showers/isolated \nt-storms Sunday afternoon. The latest HREF shows mean SBCAPEs \nmainly below 500 J/kg which will limit updraft heights with 0-6 \nkm shear 30-35 KTS. Not anticipating any severe thunderstorms at\nthis point with a slight uptick in humidity levels with \ndewpoints in the mid 50s to lower/mid 60s with highs in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s in the valleys and upper 60s to mid 70s \nover the higher terrain with partly sunny conditions giving way \nto mostly cloudy skies in the afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA wet and unsettled pattern sets up for next week with mid and \nupper level ridging upstream over the Upper Midwest, Great Lakes\nRegion and Ontario with the high centered over the lower to mid\nMS River Valley. A cold front begins to move southward across \nupstate NY, New England and PA with the west/northwest flow \naloft. We have maintained close to the NBM for few showers \n(isolated to widely scattered) on Monday mainly south and west \nof the Capital Region. However, the cold front begins to slowly \nlift back north/northeast, as a warm front Mon night into Tue \nmorning with increasing isentropic lift, and PWATs rising 1 to 2\nSTDEVs above normal. WPC has a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) in \nthe Day 3 ERO across the region. The locations for any locally \nheavy rainfall varies in the guidance and ensembles, so low \nconfidence exists and with some dry and rain free days before\nMonday, so the threat looks lower for flash flooding. \n\nTue through Wed...Multiple short-waves in the west to northwest\nflow over the top of the upstream ridge could bring showers and\nscattered thunderstorms. Some of the thunderstorms could be on\nthe strong to severe side, as the old SPC Day 4 to 8 graphic \nhighlights 15% probabilities of a severe threat for Wed-Wed \nnight with strong mid-level jet aloft of 50 KT or greater, \ndewpoints in the 60s, ample deep shear (30-40 KT) and \ninstability on the moderate to potentially large side on some \nof the medium range guidance and ensembles. The area highlighted\nwith the 15% probs is south and west of the Capital Region. \nVery challenging to time any severe threat this far out. We will\ncontinue to monitor trends over the next several days. For Tue \nand Wed, WPC has a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) in the ERO for \nan isolated flash threat with periods of locally heavy rainfall\nwith any MCS. The latest 24-hr NBM probabilities for >1\" of \nrainfall across eastern NY and western New England by 12Z/Thu \nare in the 25-50% range. Timing and placements of the \ndisturbances still vary, which makes the rainfall totals \nchallenging to pinpoint with low confidence this far out. Stayed\nclose to the NBM/WPC guidance with PoPs in the 55-70% range Tue\ninto Wed with a slight to low chance of thunderstorms. Max \ntemps Tue through Thu will be seasonable in the 70s to lower 80s\nwith cooler temps over the higher terrain with lows in the 50s \nand 60s at night.",
